<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International English</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How English Is Used in Workplaces Globally</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Share future career plans and aspirations. Interactive: Lead a discussion on various areas including academic topics or past experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can share future career aspirations, explaining the role English will play (e.g., "I want to work in diplomacy, so learning English is essential.")<br>Can lead discussions about the role of English in global workplaces, prompting classmates to share perspectives (e.g., "Why do you think English is important in international business?")<br>Can clarify classmates' points in discussions on global English, ensuring understanding (e.g., "So, you believe English helps people connect across cultures?")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a "Think-Pair-Share" activity. Teacher displays a provocative image of a diverse global workplace. Students are asked: "How has English become a *lingua franca* for *cross-cultural communication* in this image?" They think for 1 minute, share their initial thoughts with a partner for 2 minutes, then a few pairs share with the class. This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary related to *global workplaces* and *communication*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> Teacher models how to express a career aspiration and explain the role of English, using target grammar. E.g., "I want to work in *diplomacy*; English *has become* absolutely *necessary* for *global interactions*. I *must* practice my *fluency* to connect with people from different *backgrounds*." Teacher models the pronunciation of /e/ in "necessary" and "relevant," and the reduced vowel in "communication" /kəˌmjuː.nɪˈkeɪ.ʃən/. The teacher demonstrates asking an indirect question: "Can you explain which *skills* can help you advance your *career*?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b><br>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of job did I just mention?" (Checks understanding of 'diplomacy' and context.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What grammatical tense did I use to talk about English's journey to becoming a global language?" (Checks for present perfect recognition.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International English."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio interview excerpts (2-3 minutes) of young professionals from different countries discussing "How English has become *vital* in their *global workplaces*." The excerpts feature natural speech, including examples of Present Perfect, Modals, and Indirect Questions as discussed in previous lessons. Vocabulary includes words like *business, resources, online platforms, employees, access, enhance, explore, employ*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interacts in spontaneous conversations on familiar topics and on topics of personal interest.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Speaking-focused):</b> Students listen to the audio. After the first listening, they orally discuss in pairs:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What main industries did the professionals mention where English is *important*?" (Gist question)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which speaker talked about English helping them *connect* with clients from different *backgrounds*?" (Specific information)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why do you think English *has spread* so quickly in these *contexts*?" (Inferential, prompting opinion)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the Present Perfect, Modals for necessity ("must," "could," "should," "can"), and Indirect Questions to discuss the role of English in global *workplaces* and *career aspirations*. Key vocabulary: *English, workplaces, communication, skills, fluency, business, diplomacy, lingua franca, global, interactions, accessible, relevant, necessary, important, enhance, explain, connect, employ*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b><br>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Expansion:</b> Teacher provides sentence starters. In pairs, students orally expand them using Present Perfect and Modals. E.g., "English ______ (become) a _______ (global) language." (Expected: "English *has become* a *global* language.") "To *enhance* your *communication skills*, you _______ (practice)." (Expected: "To *enhance* your *communication skills*, you *should* practice.") Students repeat sentences aloud, focusing on the pronunciation of /e/ in "relevant" and "necessary."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Aspiration Dialogue:</b> Students work in new pairs. Partner A asks Partner B about their future career plans using a direct question, e.g., "What job do you want in the future?" Partner B responds, then asks an indirect question about the role of English. E.g., "I want to *explore* a career in *international business*. Could you tell me how English *has spread* so widely in that field?" Partner A then uses modals to suggest *skills* or *resources*. E.g., "You *could* take *online courses* to *enhance* your *fluency*. It *must* be *important* to have *access* to *academic resources*."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Peer Clarification:</b> Pairs engage in a short discussion (3-4 minutes) about a specific area of English use in *workplaces* (e.g., *diplomacy* vs. *tech*). One student makes a statement or expresses an opinion about why English is *relevant*. Their partner must then clarify the point using an indirect question to ensure understanding. E.g., "So, you believe English helps people *connect* across *cultures*?" or "Could you *explain* what you mean by 'English being a *dominant lingua franca*?'" Teacher circulates, monitoring for correct use of grammar and vocabulary, and noting pronunciation of full/reduced vowels in words like "communication" /kəˌmuː.nɪˈkeɪ.ʃən/ and "accessible" /əkˈses.ə.bəl/.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International English."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Global Career Panel Simulation." Students work in groups of 3-4. Each group acts as a "panel" discussing the role of English in various *global workplaces*. Each student takes on the role of a professional (e.g., diplomat, international *business* consultant, *online platforms* manager, researcher). They *share* their future *career aspirations* or past experiences in their chosen role, explaining why English *has become* so *important* and what *skills* are *necessary*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students *lead discussions* within their group, prompting classmates with questions like, "Why do you think English is *vital* in *cross-cultural communication* for a diplomat?" They also *clarify* classmates' points, ensuring *understanding* (e.g., "Are you saying *fluency* *could* *enhance* someone's ability to *connect* with diverse *backgrounds*?"). They *must* use formal language appropriate for *workplace contexts* and *recognize cross-cultural communication needs*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students are encouraged to use a wide range of vocabulary (e.g., *global, international, dominant, relevant, necessary, important, formal, fluent, communicative, cross-cultural, recognized, linguistic, wide-ranging, accessible, vital, proficient, interactive*) and the target grammar structures naturally.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the "Global Career Panel Simulation," students are assessed by the teacher using an observation checklist for speaking. Additionally, each student uses a simplified peer assessment checklist to evaluate at least one group member on their participation and use of English.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking):</b><br>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target sounds (/e/ and full/reduced vowels in multisyllabic words).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of Present Perfect and Modals for necessity.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of Indirect Questions to lead/clarify discussions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in sharing career aspirations and explaining the role of English.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation in leading discussions and prompting classmates.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to clarify classmates' points to ensure understanding.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary related to *workplaces* and *global communication*.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of formal language in *workplace contexts* and recognition of *cross-cultural communication* needs.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences orally or in writing:<br>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about English in *global workplaces* today is..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel confident/less confident using [specific grammar point: e.g., indirect questions] when I speak, because..."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b><br>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's discussion helped you understand better how English is used in different career *contexts*?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you continue to *practice* your English *communication skills* outside of class to *enhance* your future *career aspirations*?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The "Global Career Panel Simulation" effectively encouraged students to *lead discussions*, *share aspirations*, and *clarify points*. Most students confidently used the Present Perfect and Modals, though some struggled with the correct inversion for indirect questions. The pronunciation practice of /e/ was successful, but more focus on reduced vowels in longer words like "accessible" will be needed. I noticed students were highly engaged when discussing their own *career aspirations*. For next time, I could provide more sentence frames for indirect questions to scaffold students who need it and incorporate a short warm-up on question formation. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson? I need to ensure the writing task in Lesson 4 provides clear sentence starters and model paragraphs that incorporate these complex structures and vocabulary for successful written production.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about *career aspirations* and the role of English. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on this by having students write a paragraph or short essay describing their own *future career plans* and explaining in writing how English *will be necessary* for their success in *global workplaces*, using the target grammar and vocabulary we practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>